Hello,

I coordinate a volunteer centre run by a local charity, and our trustees have=
 asked me to look
into a better way of signing people in. One of our volunteers used your kiosk=
 at the food bank
and suggested I get in touch. Here's a bit about us first.

The centre. We're in a converted chapel with a hall, two meeting rooms and a =
small kitchen. We're
open six days a week. About 250 volunteers are registered with us, and on a t=
ypical day between
30 and 50 come in: some for a two-hour shift sorting donations, some for a wh=
ole day running the
advice desk, and some just for a training session.

Visitors. As well as volunteers, we get people dropping off donations, staff =
from partner
charities, and the occasional inspector from our funders. We'd want all of th=
em to sign in, but
they don't all need the same thing.

What we use now. A ring binder at the front door with a sheet for each day. V=
olunteers are meant
to write their name and the time they arrive and leave. About half remember t=
o sign out, so the
sheets are a mess by the end of the month, and our office volunteer spends a =
morning each month
trying to make sense of them.

Why it matters. Our biggest funder asks us every quarter how many volunteer h=
ours we've
delivered. At the moment we estimate. The trustees would like a real number, =
and the funder has
hinted that a more reliable figure would help our next application.

Our setup. We have broadband and one ageing laptop in the office. We can't af=
ford a lot of
hardware, and the trustees will want to know about running costs as well as t=
he price of a
kiosk. None of us is technical, so it would need to be something a volunteer =
can look after.

Accessibility. A number of our volunteers are older, and a few have learning =
disabilities. Large
text and simple steps would be essential. Some would struggle with typing a l=
ong email address.

Fire safety. Like everyone, we'd like to know who is in the building if the a=
larm goes off.
The binder is no help with that.

Volunteers who move around. Some of our volunteers work at two or three of ou=
r partner
charities during the week, and come to us for one shift. Others sign in, go o=
ut on a delivery
round, and come back. We'd want the hours to reflect the time they're actuall=
y working for us,
or at least be close enough that the funder would accept them. Our current bi=
nder can't show any
of that, and the trustees know it.

Timing. Nothing is urgent. The trustees meet in January, and I'd like to brin=
g them one option.

The question that would decide it for us: can the kiosk record how many hours=
 each volunteer is
on site, so we can report the total each month?

With thanks,
Bea Okonjo
Volunteer coordinator, Riverbank Volunteer Centre
